<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallia Narbonensis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 121 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Provincia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gallia</a> Narbonensis was the Roman province of southern Gaul between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, formed after Roman campaigns against the Allobroges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a> around 121 BCE and named after its capital, Narbo. The Gallic peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/languedoc">Languedoc</a> and the Greek city of Massalia came under it. Its name survives in the region of Provence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallia-narbonensis.jpg" alt="Gallia Narbonensis" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Milenioscuro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Empire_-_Narbonensis_(125_AD).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Roman Republic destroyed a Gallic army at Telamon in 225 BCE, conquered Cisalpine Gaul by the early 2nd century BCE, fought the Celtiberians until the fall of Numantia in 133 BCE and made southern Gaul the province of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b> about 121 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Their land was then absorbed into <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/albici">Albici</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome defeated them in 121 BCE, and their land became part of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/allobroges">Allobroges</a></li><li><blockquote>Their territory later lay within the Roman province of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/deciates">Deciates</a></li><li><blockquote>In 118 BCE Rome founded the colony of Narbo Martius next to Montlaurès, which became the capital of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/elisyces">Elisyces</a></li><li><blockquote>A steady ally of the Roman Republic, Massalia called in Roman armies against the Salyes from 125 BCE, opening the way to the province of <b>Gallia Narbonensis</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/massalia">Massalia</a></li></ul></section>
